Jahshaka patch 0060 — the bounce injection's cross-cascade bindings, re-asserted
per dispatch.

SOURCE patch. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after
pulling this. It overlaps patches 0037, 0050 and 0057 in
`Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/Vct/OgreVctLighting.cpp` (different functions) and
0050 in the header; they apply in stack order, and a per-patch reverse-check on
a tree that already carries them reports this one "unapplied" for those files —
judge by content (CLAUDE.md, the overlap blind spot).

THE DEFECT. `VctLighting::runBounce()` ends with
`std::swap( mLightVoxel[0], mLightBounce )` and re-binds slot 2 — its OWN light
voxel — immediately after. Slots 3..N are the EXTRA CASCADES' `mLightVoxel[]`
and are written ONCE, in `setupBounceTextures()`, and never again. Every one of
those cascades runs its own `runBounce()` and swaps its own pointers, so after
an ODD number of bounce iterations on a cascade this job reads the texture that
cascade has just stopped writing: the cross-cascade term of the bounce
integrates stale radiance, with no log line, no validation error and no
difference in cost — the only symptom is light that is a bounce behind where it
should be, and only in the volumes outside the one being injected.

An EVEN number of iterations happens to come back to where it started (a swap is
an involution), which is why this survived: upstream's own `VctCascadedVoxelizer`
gives every cascade the SAME bounce count. A per-cascade count is not exotic —
the stabilisation upstream itself documents and implements (a coarser cascade
gets more bounces, `OgreVctCascadedVoxelizer.cpp:465-491`) resolves to 1 / 3 / 7
on a four-cascade chain at three total bounces, which is three odd counts out of
three. Jahshaka reaches it through Photon's Epic tier (PHOTON_SPEC §7 E2);
upstream reaches it the moment anyone varies the count per cascade.

SECOND DEFECT, THE SAME HUNK CLOSES IT (patch 0057's recorded residual, and the
shared-job class of patch 0037's defect 2). "VCT/LightVctBounceInject" is found
BY NAME, so every `VctLighting` in the process shares ONE `HlmsComputeJob` while
its bindings belong to whichever instance called `setupBounceTextures()` last.
Two live cascade chains — two scenes with Photon cascades — would therefore
inject one chain's voxels through the other's textures. Re-asserting the
bindings in `runBounce()` makes them belong to the instance that is DISPATCHING,
which is the only instance that can be right about them. (Jahshaka binds VCT
process-wide to one scene through `sVctBindingOwner`, so this half was not
reachable today; it is closed because it is the same three lines.)

THE FIX, and why it is cheap enough to run per dispatch. `setupBounceTextures`
gains a `bSetSamplerRefs` parameter (default true, so every existing caller is
unchanged) that skips the OpenGL-only samplerblock reference counting — the
samplers do not move, only the textures do, and re-adding a reference per
dispatch would leak one per bounce under GL. What is left is the slot writes
themselves: the texture UNIT COUNT is change-guarded
(`getNumTexUnits() != numNeededTexUnits`), the GLSL unit list is change-guarded
inside `setupGlslTextureUnits()`, and the UAV is re-asserted to the same
`mLightBounce` `runBounce` sets after its own swap. This is the fix upstream's
own code comments point at, and patch 0057's header named it as the robust one.

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` on a tree carrying 0001-0059 and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane
worktree; `--engine-selftest` unchanged by this patch alone (the default scene
runs one bounce, where `cascadeBounces` is 0 for every cascade and no swap ever
happens). The suite is `gi.cascade_bounce_bindings`
(tests/gi/test_gi_cascade_bounce_bindings.cpp): a four-cascade chain at THREE
total bounces — the per-cascade counts 1/3/7, all odd — must light a surface
beyond cascade 0 the same way the same chain at ONE bounce does, within the
margin three bounces of extra light can explain, and must not drift further from
it frame after frame. It reds on the pre-patch pin.

